content:
The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (parent && !parent->power.ignore_children) {
    spin_unlock(&dev->power.lock);
    spin_lock(&parent->power.lock);
    rpm_idle(parent, RPM_ASYNC);
    spin_unlock(&parent->power.lock);
    spin_lock(&dev->power.lock);
}
Reason:
In `pm_runtime_work()`, after releasing `dev->power.lock`, a concurrent device removal can free the parent device. The code then tries to lock `parent->power.lock` and call `rpm_idle()` on the already freed parent, causing a use‑after‑free.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
void pm_runtime_remove(struct device *dev)
{
    __pm_runtime_disable(dev, false);
    flush_work(&dev->power.work);
    pm_runtime_reinit(dev);
}
Reason:
`flush_work(&dev->power.work)` ensures that any pending or running `pm_runtime_work()` completes before the device’s runtime PM state is reinitialized. This prevents concurrent access to the parent device after it may have been freed.

